### What are @QueryHints?

The `@QueryHints` annotation on a repository method passes optimization hints to Hibernate. These hints are applied to the specific Query object, not to the entire transaction.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import jakarta.persistence.QueryHint;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.QueryHints;
import java.util.List;

public interface TradeRepository extends JpaRepository<Trade, String> {

    @QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.readOnly", value = "true"))
    List<Trade> findByStatus(String status);
    // Entities from THIS query: no snapshot, no dirty checking
}
```

### How does the readOnly hint work?

The readOnly hint tells Hibernate to skip snapshot storage and dirty checking for entities loaded by this specific query. Other queries in the same transaction still have full dirty checking.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import jakarta.persistence.QueryHint;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.QueryHints;
import java.util.List;

public interface TradeRepository extends JpaRepository<Trade, String> {

    @QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.readOnly", value = "true"))
    List<Trade> findByStatus(String status);
    // Entities loaded here: NO snapshot, NO dirty checking
    // Saves memory and CPU for large result sets
}
```

### How does the cacheable hint work?

The cacheable hint stores the query result in Hibernate's second-level query cache. The first call executes SQL; subsequent identical calls return the cached result without a database hit.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import jakarta.persistence.QueryHint;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.QueryHints;
import java.util.List;

public interface TradeRepository extends JpaRepository<Trade, String> {

    @QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.cacheable", value = "true"))
    List<Trade> findBySymbol(String symbol);
    // First call: SQL executes, result cached in L2 query cache
    // Next identical call: returns cached result, NO database hit
    // Requires: hibernate.cache.use_query_cache=true in configuration
}
```

```yaml
# application.yml: enable query cache
spring:
  jpa:
    properties:
      hibernate:
        cache:
          use_second_level_cache: true
          use_query_cache: true
          region:
            factory_class: org.hibernate.cache.jcache.JCacheRegionFactory
```

### How does the fetchSize hint work?

The fetchSize hint controls how many rows the JDBC driver fetches per network round-trip. For large result sets, increasing fetchSize reduces round-trips.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import jakarta.persistence.QueryHint;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.QueryHints;
import java.util.List;

public interface TradeRepository extends JpaRepository<Trade, String> {

    @QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.fetchSize", value = "200"))
    List<Trade> findByAccountId(String accountId);
    // JDBC driver fetches 200 rows per network round-trip
    // Default is often 10-50
    // For 10,000 rows: fetchSize=10 = 1000 round-trips
    //                  fetchSize=200 = 50 round-trips
}
```

### How does the timeout hint work?

The timeout hint cancels queries that exceed a time limit. This prevents runaway queries from holding database connections indefinitely.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import jakarta.persistence.QueryHint;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.QueryHints;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.List;

public interface TradeRepository extends JpaRepository<Trade, String> {

    @QueryHints(@QueryHint(
        name = "jakarta.persistence.query.timeout", value = "5000"))
    List<Trade> findBySettlementDateBetween(LocalDate from, LocalDate to);
    // If query takes > 5000ms: QueryTimeoutException thrown
    // Prevents connection pool exhaustion from slow queries
}
```

### How is @QueryHints(readOnly) different from @Transactional(readOnly=true)?

`@Transactional(readOnly=true)` applies to the entire transaction – all queries skip dirty checking, and you cannot update any entity. `@QueryHints(readOnly=true)` applies to one query – only entities from that method skip dirty checking. Other queries in the same transaction work normally.

| Scope | @Transactional(readOnly=true) | @QueryHints(readOnly=true) |
|-------|------------------------------|---------------------------|
| Applies to | Entire transaction | Single query |
| Dirty checking | Skipped for ALL entities | Skipped for THIS query's entities |
| JDBC hint | Connection hinted read-only | No connection hint |
| Write safety | Writes silently ignored | Writes still work on other entities |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Trade;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import com.bank.repository.TradeRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.time.LocalDate;

@Service
public class PortfolioReportService {

    private final TradeRepository tradeRepo;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public PortfolioReportService(TradeRepository tradeRepo,
                                   AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.tradeRepo = tradeRepo;
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional  // NOT readOnly: this transaction writes
    public void generateAndStamp(String accountId) {
        // Trades loaded with @QueryHints(readOnly=true)
        var trades = tradeRepo.findByStatus("SETTLED");
        // These Trade entities: NO snapshot, NO dirty checking

        // Account loaded normally (full dirty checking)
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        account.setLastReportDate(LocalDate.now());
        // This Account IS dirty-checked. UPDATE generated.

        // If @Transactional(readOnly=true) were used instead:
        // account.setLastReportDate() would be SILENTLY IGNORED!
    }
}
```

### How do you combine multiple hints?

Use `@QueryHints` with an array of `@QueryHint` annotations to apply multiple hints to one method.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Trade;
import jakarta.persistence.QueryHint;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Pageable;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.QueryHints;
import java.util.List;

public interface TradeRepository extends JpaRepository<Trade, String> {

    @QueryHints({
        @QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.readOnly", value = "true"),
        @QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.fetchSize", value = "200"),
        @QueryHint(name = "jakarta.persistence.query.timeout", value = "10000")
    })
    List<Trade> findLargeDataset(Pageable pageable);
    // readOnly: no snapshots
    // fetchSize: 200 rows per round-trip
    // timeout: cancel if > 10 seconds
}
```

### What are common mistakes with @QueryHints?

The most common mistake is confusing `@QueryHints(readOnly)` with `@Transactional(readOnly=true)` and expecting writes to work.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class QueryHintMistake {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public QueryHintMistake(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // WRONG: @Transactional(readOnly=true) but method writes
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public void wrongReadOnly(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        account.setBalance(amount);
        // Balance change is SILENTLY IGNORED! (readOnly TX)
    }

    // CORRECT: use @QueryHints(readOnly) for selective read-only
    @Transactional  // writable transaction
    public void correctSelectiveReadOnly(String accountId) {
        // Trades loaded readOnly via @QueryHints on the repo method
        var trades = accountRepo.findAll();  // readOnly hint on this method

        // Account can still be modified
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        account.setBalance(new BigDecimal("400000.00"));
        // UPDATE generated (writable entity, writable TX)
    }
}
```
